findings.body:
Password-reset tokens must be unguessable. This replaces `randomBytes(32)` (256 bits of CSPRNG entropy) with `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)`, which is both non-cryptographic and limited to at most 10^12 distinct values (~40 bits), then padded to a ~9-character string.

An attacker who can request resets and try tokens can brute-force or predict values and take over accounts. Edge runtimes cannot use Node's `crypto.randomBytes`, but they do provide Web Crypto `crypto.getRandomValues`, which is the correct portable replacement.

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```
